2026-09-02T10:55Z claude note PLATFORM-SIDE CORRECTIONS, seven sites, and two of them were the audit's worst evidence grades. Wrong mechanism: C1's '/fulltext?since= counter, which Zotero bumps when it re-extracts synced content' -- the counter carries a SYNC version, the server stamps it, and local extraction writes 0 outright (REPLACE INTO fulltextItems (itemID, version, synced) VALUES (?, 0, ?), fulltext.js at 9e28eb0). Wrong scope: 'different versions, different keys' -- item keys are sync keys, UNIQUE (libraryID, key), identical across two installs of one account, so they distinguish nothing; the glossary gloss carried the same error. '/file/view/url redirect' -- that route answers with the URL as a plain-text body, the 302 is /file and /file/view. 'The local API has no rate limits' -- true of every data endpoint, false of /api/local/authorize at 5 per 60 s. Upstream #39 'chose a Web-API fallback' -- it chose a per-API concurrency default, 2 desktop against 4 cloud, with back-off. Section 6's 'a keyless install fails such a read rather than sending it anywhere' -- it fails AT THE SERVER; the request is dispatched to api.zotero.org under user id 0. No library content crosses, which is the substantive point, but a request does and the sentence denied it -- in the security section, which is where that matters most. Wrong grade: 'the documented deletion route' is documented nowhere, the inference is ours. make check green
2026-09-02T10:56Z claude note SELF-CORRECTION, and it is the audit's sharpest lesson. Two hours ago this ticket logged that C1's residue bullet was VERIFIED verbatim, on the strength of the comment at embeddings.js:2337-2345, and the coordinate was committed. That verdict is WRONG and is now reversed. The comment is stale: commit 57b30b17e (2026-08-20, inside #6012, not on main) folded the CURRENT processor version into the attachment staleness key -- md5(path|size|lastModified|processorVersion), where getProcessorVersion reads SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS from the module metadata rather than the pack's own stamp (sdt.js:298-308) -- and _getAttachmentChunks calls getSections(item.id, {allowStale: false}) at embeddings.js:2428, which per sdt.js:60-72 WAITS for regeneration instead of returning the stale pack. So at the reviewed head Zotero chases a processor bump with no file change, and the precedent runs the OPPOSITE way to what C1 claimed. The 2026-08-31 ratified withdrawal reaches the right verdict; its stated reason -- that embeddings.js addresses the case nowhere -- is also wrong, because the file addresses it squarely and resolves it. So there is nothing to put to the author after all: C1's bullet is corrected here, which APPLIES the ratified withdrawal to the site it never reached, and the awaiting entry drafted for a reversal is not filed. The class this ticket names has a third face: not a constant adopted without its guard, and not a rationale graded as a measurement, but A COMMENT CITED AS BEHAVIOUR while the code beneath it says otherwise. My own first read fell into it, which is the strongest evidence the class is real
